WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.990
Many AI systems depend on data pipelines before inference ever happens.

2
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:16.470
A model-serving issue may actually begin with a failed ingestion job, a delayed index update, stale content, missing embeddings, or a retrieval source that is unavailable.

3
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:23.270
Data and retrieval telemetry helps teams avoid treating every output problem as a model problem.

4
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:33.190
Retrieval-augmented systems need evidence about ingestion status, document processing, indexing success, embedding job progress, vector database health, retrieval latency, and result availability.

5
00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:40.770
Teams also need to know which index, corpus, data source, or content version participated in a request.

6
00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:47.570
That context makes it possible to connect user-facing behavior to the underlying knowledge source.

7
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.620
Freshness and lifecycle status matter.

8
00:00:50.870 --> 00:01:00.930
If content is stale, deprecated, partially indexed, or waiting on approval, the system may return less reliable results while all infrastructure dashboards look healthy.

9
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:11.240
Telemetry should surface when data moved, when it was validated, when it became available for retrieval, and when it should no longer be used.

10
00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.530
Permission-aware retrieval adds another requirement.

11
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:23.280
Teams need evidence that access decisions were made, but logs should not expose protected document contents or sensitive source material.

12
00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:31.640
Batch jobs and streaming pipelines also need failure counts, retry behavior, backlog size, processing latency, and data quality indicators.

13
00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:38.440
Pipeline telemetry helps teams separate model behavior from data availability, indexing, retrieval, and governance issues.

